diff --git a/README_MULTI_LIBRETRO.md b/README_MULTI_LIBRETRO.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..31ef548
--- /dev/null
+++ b/README_MULTI_LIBRETRO.md
@@ -0,0 +1,139 @@
+# mGBA Multi libretro core
+
+This fork adds a separate `mGBA Multi` libretro target. It keeps the normal
+mGBA libretro core unchanged and places one, two, or three emulated systems in
+a stable widescreen layout.
+
+## Features
+
+- One to three local instances, with two as the default.
+- Independent controllers on RetroArch ports 1, 2, and 3.
+- Independent speed targets from 1x to 3x in 0.25x steps for every instance,
+  with an R2 toggle on each player's controller.
+- One mixed audio stream, or audio from a selected player.
+- Separate battery saves for every instance.
+- One combined RetroArch save state containing every running instance.
+- RetroArch subsystems for loading two or three different ROMs. Normal
+  **Load Content** duplicates the selected ROM for the configured number of
+  instances.
+
+Version 0.4.0 removes link-cable emulation and all worker-thread paths. Every
+instance executes directly on RetroArch's core thread, and the target is built
+with the same `DISABLE_THREADING` configuration as the standard mGBA libretro
+core.
+
+## Build
+
+```sh
+cmake -S . -B build-multi \
+  -DBUILD_LIBRETRO_MULTI=ON \
+  -DBUILD_LIBRETRO=OFF \
+  -DBUILD_QT=OFF \
+  -DBUILD_SDL=OFF \
+  -DENABLE_SCRIPTING=OFF
+cmake --build build-multi --target mgba_multi_libretro --parallel
+```
+
+The result is `mgba_multi_libretro.so` on Linux,
+`mgba_multi_libretro.dll` on Windows, or
+`mgba_multi_libretro.dylib` on macOS. Copy it to RetroArch's cores
+directory. Copy `src/platform/libretro/mgba_multi_libretro.info` to
+RetroArch's info directory if you want local metadata.
+
+### tvOS
+
+On macOS with Xcode and the Apple TV SDK installed, build an arm64 tvOS core
+with:
+
+```sh
+cmake -S . -B build-tvos -G Xcode \
+  -DCMAKE_SYSTEM_NAME=tvOS \
+  -DCMAKE_OSX_SYSROOT=appletvos \
+  -DCMAKE_OSX_ARCHITECTURES=arm64 \
+  -DCMAKE_OSX_DEPLOYMENT_TARGET=13.0 \
+  -DBUILD_LIBRETRO_MULTI=ON \
+  -DBUILD_LIBRETRO=OFF \
+  -DBUILD_QT=OFF \
+  -DBUILD_SDL=OFF \
+  -DBUILD_GL=OFF \
+  -DBUILD_GLES2=OFF \
+  -DBUILD_GLES3=OFF \
+  -DENABLE_SCRIPTING=OFF \
+  -DENABLE_DEBUGGERS=OFF \
+  -DDISABLE_DEPS=ON \
+  -DSKIP_LIBRARY=ON
+cmake --build build-tvos --config Release \
+  --target mgba_multi_libretro --parallel
+```
+
+The output is a device-only arm64 dylib. RetroArch's Apple build converts it
+to an embedded framework before packaging the tvOS app.
+
+## Use
+
+1. Load the **mGBA Multi** core.
+2. Open **Quick Menu > Core Options**.
+3. Set **Instances** to 1, 2, or 3.
+4. Set **Player 1 speed target**, **Player 2 speed target**, and **Player 3
+   speed target** as needed.
+5. Use **Close Content**, then load the content again after changing the
+   instance count. RetroArch's **Restart** command only resets the already-
+   running instances. Speed changes apply immediately.
+6. Assign controllers to RetroArch ports 1 through 3.
+
+Press **R2** on a player's controller to toggle only that player between 1x
+and the configured target. R2 is edge-triggered, so holding it does not cycle
+or repeatedly toggle the speed. Every content session starts at 1x, even when
+a faster target is configured. Set a target above 1x, then press R2 to activate
+it; press R2 again to return to 1x. Changing a target does not activate it.
+
+Loading one ROM through **Load Content** runs that ROM in every instance. To
+use different games or versions, use RetroArch's **Load Subsystem** flow and
+choose either **mGBA Multi (2 ROMs)** or **mGBA Multi (3 ROMs)**.
+
+## Screen layout and speed
+
+Player placement is deterministic and remains tied to the same controller and
+save slot after content is restarted:
+
+| Player | Placement with three instances | Save slot |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| 1 | Top-left | RetroArch's normal save |
+| 2 | Top-right | `.p2.srm` |
+| 3 | Bottom-center | `.p3.srm` |
+
+The core pads the native-resolution layout toward 16:9 without reducing the
+largest uniform scale that fits the display. It never stretches an individual
+screen or changes its aspect ratio. With two instances, Player 1 stays on the
+left and Player 2 stays on the right.
+
+Individual speed targets advance only the selected instance. Targets are
+available in 0.25x steps from 1x through 3x, and each controller's R2 button
+toggles its own target on or off. Audio from a sped-up instance is resampled
+into the normal frontend time window.
+
+All instances run directly and extra frames are scheduled round-robin. Video,
+audio, and periodic saves are collected only after every active instance
+reaches its frame boundary. GBA audio uses the same moving-average pacing as
+the standard mGBA libretro core. Each audio queue has a fixed hard limit and a
+one-frame carry limit, so neither queued data nor per-frame work can grow over
+time. The compositor clears widescreen padding only when the layout changes,
+and the common 1x audio path avoids resampling work.
+
+## Save files
+
+Player 1 uses RetroArch's normal save-memory interface, so RetroArch creates
+its ordinary `.srm` file. The core owns the extra files:
+
+| Player | Save file |
+| --- | --- |
+| 1 | RetroArch's normal `<content>.srm` |
+| 2 | `<ROM stem>.p2.srm` |
+| 3 | `<ROM stem>.p3.srm` |
+
+Player 2 and 3 saves are written in RetroArch's save directory every 300
+frames and when content is unloaded. A RetroArch save state is a fixed
+container that stores all active instances together; it must be loaded with
+the same instance count.
+
+This fork retains mGBA's Mozilla Public License 2.0 licensing.
